{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nThe user is asking me to implement a `solve(case)` function for task SE-PE-01. Let me analyze the fixture data to understand what the task requires.\n\nLooking at the fixtures:\n- Each case has a tree structure with nodes having: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n- There's a `required_symbols` list\n- The task seems to involve selecting/pruning nodes from a tree\n\nLet me think about what SE-PE-01 might be asking. Looking at the data:\n- There's a tree rooted at \"root\"\n- Each node has a `removable` flag and a `utility` value\n- There are `required_symbols` that must be present\n\nThe task seems to be about pruning the tree while:\n1. Keeping nodes whose symbols are in `required_symbols` (or they're not removable)\n2. Maximizing total utility\n3. Or finding some optimal subset\n\nWait, I need to think more carefully. The task is \"SE-PE-01\" which might stand for \"Symbol-based Pruning/Elimination\". \n\nLooking at the structure:\n- Nodes that are `removable: false` cannot be removed\n- Nodes that are `removable: true` can potentially be removed\n- `required_symbols` are symbols that must be present in the final tree\n\nLet me think about what the output should be. It probably returns a list of node IDs to keep or remove.\n\nGiven the required_symbols are [\"root\", \"s1\"] in all cases, and looking at the nodes:\n- root: symbol=\"root\", removable=false\n- n1: symbol=\"s1\", removable=false\n- n2: symbol=\"s2\", removable=true\n- n3: symbol=\"s0\", removable=false\n- n4: symbol=\"s1\", removable=true (child of n2)\n- n5: symbol=\"s2\", removable=false (child of n3)\n- n6: symbol=\"s0\", removable=true (child of n1)\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1\n\nSo nodes with required symbols must be kept. The question is which removable nodes to keep/remove to maximize utility while satisfying constraints.\n\nActually, I think the task might be simpler: identify which nodes should be removed (the removable ones that don't have required symbols in their subtrees, or something like that).\n\nLet me reconsider. Looking at this as a tree pruning problem:\n- We can remove nodes that are `removable=true`\n- But if we remove a node, we also remove its subtree (or need to handle orphans)\n- We must keep at least one node for each symbol in `required_symbols`\n- We want to maximize total utility of remaining nodes\n\nActually, thinking about it differently - maybe the task is to find the optimal subset of removable nodes to remove, maximizing some objective.\n\nOr maybe the output is: given the constraints, which nodes should be kept?\n\nLet me think about what makes sense as output:\n1. List of node IDs to keep\n2. List of node IDs to remove\n3. Total utility of optimal solution\n4. Some combination\n\nSince I don't have the expected outputs, I need to infer from the problem description and fixture shapes. The task is registered as SE-PE-01 in \"registry_id: B\".\n\nGiven the name \"SE-PE\" might stand for \"Symbol-constrained Pruning/Elimination\" or something similar...\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The problem says:\n- `required_symbols`: symbols that must appear in the surviving tree\n- `removable`: whether a node can be removed\n\nA reasonable interpretation:\n- We want to find the minimum cost (or maximum utility retained) set of nodes to keep\n- We must keep all non-removable nodes\n- We must ensure every required symbol appears at least once\n- If we remove a node, we must also remove all its descendants (tree structure)\n- Or alternatively, removing a node just removes it and its children become orphans or get re-parented\n\nActually the most natural tree pruning interpretation: if you remove a node, all its descendants are also removed (standard subtree pruning).\n\nSo the question becomes: which subtrees to prune (by removing removable nodes) while:\n1. Keeping all non-removable nodes (you can't remove them)\n2. Ensuring all required_symbols appear in the remaining tree\n3. Some objective (maximize total utility of kept nodes, or minimize removed utility)\n\nWait, but if non-removable nodes can't be removed, and they might have removable ancestors... that's contradictory. If a parent is removable and we remove it, what happens to its non-removable children?\n\nLet me look at the tree structure more carefully for case 0:\n- root (non-removable) → n1 (s1, non-removable), n2 (s2, removable), n3 (s0, non-removable)\n- n1 → n6 (s0, removable)\n- n2 → n4 (s1, removable)\n- n3 → n5 (s2, non-removable)\n\nIf we remove n2 (removable), we'd also remove n4. But n4 is removable, so that's fine.\nIf we remove n1... but n1 is non-removable, so we can't.\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1\n- root is present at root node (non-removable) ✓\n- s1 is present at n1 (non-removable) ✓ and at n4 (removable, child of n2)\n\nSo we could remove n2 (and n4) and still satisfy required_symbols since n1 has symbol s1.\n\nActually, maybe the task is simply: find the set of removable nodes to remove that maximizes utility while keeping all required symbols covered.\n\nHmm, but the problem doesn't explicitly say we're maximizing or minimizing anything with respect to which removable nodes to keep...\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the output is just the set of nodes that survive after optimal pruning.\n\nActually, without knowing the expected output format, let me try to implement a reasonable interpretation:\n\nThe task: Return the IDs of nodes that should be KEPT after optimal pruning.\n- Non-removable nodes are always kept (but if their ancestor is removed, they can't be kept - contradiction, so maybe non-removable nodes' ancestors can't be removed)\n- Actually wait - the constraint might be: you can only remove a removable node if doing so doesn't violate:\n  1. All non-removable nodes remain in the tree (connected to root)\n  2. All required symbols are still represented\n\nIf we can only remove leaf-removable nodes or entire subtrees of removable nodes that don't contain non-removable nodes...\n\nLet me look at the tree again:\n- n2 (removable) has child n4 (removable) - entire subtree is removable, can remove n2+n4\n- n6 (removable) has no children - can remove n6\n- n1 (non-removable) - cannot remove\n- n3 (non-removable) - cannot remove\n\nSo removable nodes we might consider removing: n2 (with n4), n6\n- Required symbols: root (at root), s1 (at n1 and potentially n4)\n- If we remove n2+n4: s1 still covered by n1 ✓\n- If we remove n6: no required symbols lost ✓\n\nTo maximize utility: keep all nodes = keep n2+n4+n6\nTo minimize tree: remove everything removable that doesn't violate constraints\n\nHmm, I'm still not sure what the objective is. Let me think about this differently.\n\nMaybe the output format should be:\n```json\n{\n  \"kept_nodes\": [...],\n  \"removed_nodes\": [...],\n  \"total_utility\": ...\n}\n```\n\nOr maybe it's just:\n```json\n[\"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", ...]\n```\n\nWithout expected outputs, I need to make an assumption. Let me assume the task is:\n- Find the maximum utility subset of nodes to KEEP\n- Subject to: all non-removable nodes are kept, required symbols are covered\n- If we keep a node, we must keep all its ancestors (path to root)\n- We want to maximize total utility\n\nUnder this interpretation, all non-removable nodes must be kept. Then for removable nodes, we add them if they increase utility (they always do since utility is positive), so we'd keep all nodes.\n\nThat's trivially \"keep everything\", which doesn't make sense as a problem.\n\nAlternative: minimize the number of nodes (or total utility of removed nodes) while maintaining:\n1. Required symbol coverage\n2. Non-removable nodes stay\n3. Tree connectivity\n\nOr maybe it's the opposite: we want to MINIMIZE the tree while keeping required symbols. Remove as many nodes as possible, but:\n- Can't remove non-removable nodes\n- Must maintain tree connectivity for remaining nodes\n- Must keep at least one node for each required symbol\n\nIn this case, we'd remove all removable nodes unless doing so would violate required symbol coverage.\n\nFor case 0:\n- Required: root, s1\n- root is covered by non-removable root node\n- s1 is covered by non-removable n1\n- n2 (s2, removable): can we remove? s2 is not required. Remove n2+n4. ✓\n- n6 (s0, removable): can we remove? s0 is not required. Remove n6. ✓\n- Result: keep root, n1, n3, n5\n\nHmm, but n4 has symbol s1. If s1 is required and n1 already covers it, n4 is redundant and can be removed.\n\nThis \"minimum tree\" interpretation seems consistent. Let me go with it.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Start with all non-removable nodes as required to keep\n2. For each required symbol, check if it's covered by non-removable nodes. If not, we need to keep at least one removable node with that symbol.\n3. Remove all removable nodes that aren't needed to satisfy symbol coverage\n4. But we also need tree connectivity - if we keep a node, we need to keep its path to root\n\nWait, but non-removable nodes might be disconnected if their parent is removed... But if a parent is removable and a child is non-removable, we can't remove the parent (it would disconnect the non-removable child).\n\nSo the constraint is more like: a removable node can only be removed if:\n1. It has no non-removable descendants\n2. Its removal doesn't make any required symbol uncovered\n\nLet me reformulate:\n- A removable node is a candidate for removal if its entire subtree is removable\n- Actually, we process bottom-up: we can remove a removable leaf. We can remove a removable internal node only if all its children have been removed (or will be removed).\n- Constraint: after removals, all required symbols must still appear at least once.\n\nThis simplifies to: find a set of removable subtrees to prune such that:\n1. No non-removable node is in a pruned subtree\n2. After pruning, every required symbol appears at least once\n\nTo find the MINIMUM remaining tree (remove as much as possible):\n- For each removable node, determine if it (and its prunable subtree) can be removed without violating constraints\n- A node's subtree is \"prunable\" if it contains no non-removable nodes\n\nLet me code this up:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build lookup structures\n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # For each node, determine if its subtree is entirely removable\n    # (no non-removable nodes in subtree)\n    def subtree_all_removable(node_id):\n        node = node_by_id[node_id]\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            return False\n        return all(subtree_all_removable(c) for c in children[node_id])\n    \n    # Get all symbols covered by non-removable nodes\n    # (these are always kept)\n    symbols_covered_by_fixed = set()\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            symbols_covered_by_fixed.add(n[\"symbol\"])\n    \n    # Determine which symbols still need coverage from removable nodes\n    symbols_needing_coverage = required_symbols - symbols_covered_by_fixed\n    \n    # For removable nodes that can potentially be pruned (subtree all removable),\n    # decide whether to keep or remove based on symbol coverage needs\n    \n    # Actually, let's think about which nodes to KEEP:\n    # - All non-removable nodes: always keep\n    # - Removable nodes: keep only if needed for symbol coverage\n    #   AND if they're needed, we need their ancestors too (path to root)\n    \n    # Find removable nodes needed for coverage\n    needed_removable = set()\n    for sym in symbols_needing_coverage:\n        # Find removable nodes with this symbol that are reachable\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == sym and n[\"removable\"]]\n        # Pick the one with highest utility? Or any one?\n        # Let's pick the one with highest utility\n        if candidates:\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n            needed_removable.add(best[\"id\"])\n            # Also need to keep path to root\n            cur = best[\"parent\"]\n            while cur is not None:\n                if node_by_id[cur][\"removable\"]:\n                    needed_removable.add(cur)\n                cur = node_by_id[cur][\"parent\"]\n    \n    # Final kept set: all non-removable + needed removable\n    kept = set()\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not n[\"removable\"] or n[\"id\"] in needed_removable:\n            kept.add(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    return {\"kept_nodes\": sorted(kept), \"removed_nodes\": sorted(set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes) - kept)}\n```\n\nWait, I still don't know the expected output format. Let me look at this differently.\n\nThe fixture only shows input shapes. The task says \"The attached public fixtures define input shapes only.\" So I need to infer the algorithm from the task description name and structure.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, let me implement a reasonable and well-defined algorithm:\n\nTask: Given a tree with removable/non-removable nodes and required symbols, find which nodes to keep to maximize total utility subject to:\n1. All non-removable nodes are kept\n2. All required symbols are covered\n3. Tree connectivity (if a node is kept, its parent must also be kept - but non-removable nodes might create issues)\n\nActually, for a pruning problem, typically:\n- You can only remove subtrees (a node and all its descendants)\n- Non-removable nodes can never be removed\n- Required symbols must remain covered\n\nThe output is likely the list of kept node IDs and/or total utility.\n\nLet me implement this as: find the minimum-utility tree that satisfies all constraints (keep non-removable, keep required symbols), which means removing as many removable nodes as possible.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just implement a clean, reasonable solution and return a structured result. The most natural output for this type of problem is:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"kept\": [...node ids...],\n  \"pruned\": [...node ids...],\n  \"utility\": \u003csum of utilities of kept nodes\u003e\n}\n```\n\nLet me write the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    \n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Find root\n    root_id = next(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"parent\"] is None)\n    \n    # DFS to determine which nodes to keep\n    # A node is kept if:\n    # 1. It's non-removable, OR\n    # 2. It's needed to maintain path to a kept descendant, OR\n    # 3. Its symbol is required and not otherwise covered\n    \n    # First pass: determine coverage by non-removable nodes\n    fixed_symbols = {n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"]}\n    uncovered = required_symbols - fixed_symbols\n    \n    # We need to keep at least one removable node for each uncovered symbol\n    # Choose the highest utility one and keep its path to root\n    \n    must_keep = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"])\n    \n    for sym in uncovered:\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == sym and n[\"removable\"]]\n        if not candidates:\n            continue\n        best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n        # Keep this node and its ancestors\n        nid = best[\"id\"]\n        while nid is not None:\n            must_keep.add(nid)\n            nid = node_by_id[nid][\"parent\"]\n    \n    kept = must_keep\n    pruned = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes) - kept\n    \n    total_utility = sum(node_by_id[nid][\"utility\"] for nid in kept)\n    \n    return {\n        \"kept\": sorted(kept),\n        \"pruned\": sorted(pruned),\n        \"utility\": round(total_utility, 8)\n    }\n```\n\nActually, wait. I realize I might be overthinking this. Let me re-read the task description:\n\n\"Registered procedure task SE-PE-01\" - SE could be \"Symbol Elimination\" or \"Subset/Selection\", PE could be \"Pruning/Elimination\"\n\nThe problem asks me to implement `solve(case)` that returns a JSON-serializable object without mutating `case`.\n\nGiven the fixture data and the problem structure, I'll implement a tree pruning solution that:\n1. Always keeps non-removable nodes\n2. Ensures all required symbols are covered\n3. Prunes removable nodes that aren't needed\n4. Keeps path integrity (if keeping a node, keep path to root)\n\nLet me finalize my implementation. I'll return a dict with kept/pruned node IDs and total utility.\n\nHere's my final implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case.get(\"nodes\", [])\n    required_symbols = set(case.get(\"required_symbols\", []))\n    \n    node_by_id = {n[\"id\"]: dict(n) for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    \n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Symbols covered by non-removable nodes (always kept)\n    fixed_symbols = {n[\"symbol\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"]}\n    \n    # Required symbols not covered by non-removable nodes\n    uncovered = required_symbols - fixed_symbols\n    \n    # Start with all non-removable nodes\n    must_keep = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"])\n    \n    # For each uncovered required symbol, find the best removable node\n    for sym in sorted(uncovered):\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == sym and n[\"removable\"]]\n        if not candidates:\n            continue\n        # Pick highest utility candidate\n        best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n        # Keep this node and the entire path to root\n        nid = best[\"id\"]\n        while nid is not None:\n            must_keep.add(nid)\n            parent = node_by_id[nid][\"parent\"]\n            nid = parent\n    \n    pruned = sorted(set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes) - must_keep)\n    kept = sorted(must_keep)\n    total_utility = sum(node_by_id[nid][\"utility\"] for nid in kept)\n    \n    return {\n        \"kept_nodes\": kept,\n        \"pruned_nodes\": pruned,\n        \"total_utility\": total_utility\n    }\n```\n\nLet me trace through case 0:\n- Nodes: root(root,F), n1(s1,F), n2(s2,T), n3(s0,F), n4(s1,T), n5(s2,F), n6(s0,T)\n- Required: {root, s1}\n- Fixed symbols (non-removable): {root, s1, s0, s2} (from root, n1, n3, n5)\n- Uncovered = {root, s1} - {root, s1, s0, s2} = {} (empty!)\n- So just keep all non-removable: root, n1, n3, n5\n- Pruned: n2, n4, n6\n\nTotal utility = 1.0 + 0.33900027 + 0.12400094 + 0.42802136 = 1.89102257\n\nThis seems reasonable. The algorithm prunes all removable nodes that aren't needed to cover required symbols, since all required symbols are already covered by non-removable nodes.\n\nFor case 1 and 2, the structure is the same with different utility values but same symbols, so the result would be similar.\n\nThis is a clean, reasonable implementation. Let me format it as the required JSON response.\n\nWait, I need to be careful about the JSON encoding. The response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string.\n\nLet me write the final JSON properly, escaping all the Python source code.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes', [])\\n    required_symbols = set(case.get('required_symbols', []))\\n\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n\\n    # Symbols already covered by non-removable nodes (these are always kept)\\n    fixed_symbols = {n['symbol'] for n in nodes if not n['removable']}\\n\\n    # Required symbols not yet covered by any non-removable node\\n    uncovered = required_symbols - fixed_symbols\\n\\n    # Seed must_keep with every non-removable node\\n    must_keep = {n['id'] for n in nodes if not n['removable']}\\n\\n    # For each still-uncovered required symbol, pick the highest-utility\\n    # removable node that carries it and keep that node plus its whole\\n    # ancestor path up to (and including) the root.\\n    for sym in sorted(uncovered):\\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n['symbol'] == sym and n['removable']]\\n        if not candidates:\\n            continue\\n        best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n['utility'])\\n        nid = best['id']\\n        while nid is not None:\\n            must_keep.add(nid)\\n            nid = node_by_id[nid]['parent']\\n\\n    all_ids = {n['id'] for n in nodes}\\n    kept_nodes = sorted(must_keep)\\n    pruned_nodes = sorted(all_ids - must_keep)\\n    total_utility = sum(node_by_id[nid]['utility'] for nid in kept_nodes)\\n\\n    return {\\n        'kept_nodes': kept_nodes,\\n        'pruned_nodes': pruned_nodes,\\n        'total_utility': total_utility\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_018hD4tClhZs2w1EXZHuCulY","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":1904,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":31,"output_tokens":5682,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5186},"service_tier":"standard"}}